   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.6",
    "topic_title": "Sinusoidal Function Transformations",
    "stem": "The function f is defined by f(θ) = 4 sin(−3(θ + π/6)) + 2. What is the period of f?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Coefficient-as-period error: this reports |b| rather than the period. The coefficient controls horizontal scaling, so the period must be computed as 2π/|b|. Fix: Identify b, then divide 2π by |b|."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "6π",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Horizontal-scaling reversal: this multiplies 2π by 3 instead of dividing. A coefficient with magnitude 3 compresses each cycle horizontally to one-third its original length. Fix: Divide the original sine period by the coefficient’s magnitude."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "2π/3",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "In the form a sin(b(θ + c)) + d, the coefficient b is −3. The period is 2π/|b| = 2π/3; the vertical stretch and shifts do not change it."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "−2π/3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Missing-absolute-value error: this uses 2π/b instead of 2π/|b|. The negative coefficient reverses horizontal orientation, but a period is a positive cycle length. Fix: Take the absolute value of b before computing the period."
     }
    ]
   },
